```python
import numpy as np

def pi_energy(bonds, n_atoms, n_electrons):
    A = np.zeros((n_atoms, n_atoms))
    for i, j in bonds:
        A[i, j] = A[j, i] = 1
    x = np.sort(np.linalg.eigvalsh(A))[::-1]
    return float(2 * x[: n_electrons // 2].sum())

def localisation_energy(bonds, n_atoms, n_electrons, r):
    keep = [i for i in range(n_atoms) if i != r]
    new = {old: k for k, old in enumerate(keep)}
    bonds2 = [(new[i], new[j]) for i, j in bonds if r not in (i, j)]
    return pi_energy(bonds, n_atoms, n_electrons) - pi_energy(bonds2, n_atoms - 1, n_electrons - 2)
```

Benzene gives $2.536|\beta|$ at every position. Naphthalene gives $2.299|\beta|$ at position 1 and $2.480|\beta|$ at position 2: both below benzene (naphthalene is more reactive, as observed), and position 1 lower. The reason shows up when you draw the arenium ions: attack at position 1 gives a cation whose charge can spread round the attacked ring while the *other* ring keeps a complete benzene sextet in two resonance structures; attack at position 2 allows only one such structure. Bromination of naphthalene, for example, gives mainly 1-bromonaphthalene.
